1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,360
Good morning. In the last few lectures I've been talking about the history of domestic building construction.

2
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:14,000
But today I want to begin looking at some contemporary, experimental designs for housing.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,600
So, I'm going to start with a house

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:21,330
which is constructed more or less under the ground.

5
00:00:21,330 --> 00:00:28,103
And one of the interesting things about this project is that the owners-both professionals

6
00:00:28,103 --> 00:00:32,140
but not architects-wanted to be closely involved

7
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,360
So they decided to manage the project themselves.

8
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:43,500
Their chief aim was to create somewhere that was as environmentally-friendly as possible.

9
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:48,180
But at the same time they wanted to live somewhere peaceful

10
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:53,480
They'd both grown up in a rural area and disliked urban life.

11
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,059
So the first thing they did was to look for a site.

12
00:00:58,802 --> 00:01:02,960
And they found a disused stone quarry in a beautiful area

13
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,940
The price was relatively low

14
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:10,430
and they liked the idea of recycling the land, as it were.

15
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:16,260
As it was, the quarry was an ugly blot on the landscape

16
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:19,000
and it wasn't productive any longer, either.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:28,290
They consulted various architects and looked at a number of designs before finally deciding on one.

18
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:34,200
As I've said, it was a design for a sort of underground house,

19
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,200
and it was built into the earth itself, with two storeys.

20
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:44,784
The north, east and west sides were set in the earth,

21
00:01:44,784 --> 00:01:49,860
and only the sloping, south-facing side was exposed to light.

22
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:55,440
That was made of a double layer of very strong glass.

23
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:03,300
There were also photovoltaic tiles fixed to the top and bottom of this sloping wall.

24
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:08,270
These are tiles that are designed to store energy from the sun.

25
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:14,770
And the walls had a layer of foam around them too, to increase the insulation.

26
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:20,640
Now, what is of interest to us about this project is the features

27
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:23,130
which make the building energy-efficient.

28
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:26,277
Sunlight floods in through the glass wall,

29
00:02:26,277 --> 00:02:32,570
and to maximise it there are lots of mirrors and windows inside the house.

30
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:37,100
That helps to spread the light around. So that's the first thing

31
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,600
light is utilised as fully as possible.

32
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:46,990
In addition, the special tiles on the outside convert energy from the sun

33
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:50,430
and generate some of the house's electricity.

34
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:55,340
In fact, and it's possible that in future

35
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:57,630
the house may even generate an electricity surplus,

36
00:02:57,630 --> 00:03:03,930
and that the owners will be able to sell some to the national grid.

37
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:10,330
As well as that, wherever possible, recycled materials have been used.

38
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:15,570
For example, the floors are made of reclaimed wood.

39
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:20,420
And the owners haven't bought a single item of new furniture

40
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:23,520
They just kept what they already had.

41
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,800
And then there's the system for dealing with the waste produced in the house.

42
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,111
This is dealt with organically -

43
00:03:31,111 --> 00:03:36,000
it's purified by being filtered through reed beds

44
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:39,430
which have been planted for that purpose in the garden.

45
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:46,320
So the occupants of the house won't pollute the land or use any damaging chemicals.

46
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:53,730
It's true that the actual construction of the house was harmful to the environment.

47
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:58,330
Mainly because they had to use massive amounts of concrete -

48
00:03:58,330 --> 00:04:02,930
one of the biggest sources of carbon dioxide in manufacturing.

49
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:08,770
And, as you know, this is very damaging to the environment.

50
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:17,192
In total, the house construction has released 70 tons of carbon dioxide into the air.

51
00:04:17,192 --> 00:04:20,000
Now that's a frightening thought.

52
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,154
However, once the initial 'debt' has been cleared -

53
00:04:24,154 --> 00:04:29,867
and it's been calculated that this will only take fifteen years -

54
00:04:29,867 --> 00:04:34,540
this underground house won't cost anything - environmentally I mean -

55
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:42,330
because unlike ordinary houses, it is run in a way that is completely environmentally friendly.

56
00:04:42,330 --> 00:04:47,760
So, eco-housing like this is likely to become much more...
